import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from con_helpers import *
save(5,7,"From the Mind in Time to the Awareness Beneath It","When the gap between two thoughts shows up, look gently: is there only an absence, or is there a presence? Let what is there be what it is.",[
mc("U","What does Conversation 5.7 mean by the mind in time and the awareness beneath it?",
 ["Two separate minds, one for the day and one for the night, which never meet.","The stream of thought, feeling and breath, and the awareness it happens in.","A younger and an older part of the mind, the older of which wakes late in life.","The thinking mind, and a deeper store of memory from which thoughts are drawn."],1,
 "The same experience can be read in two ways: as the melody flowing in time, and as the deeper ground it arises within. That ground is not another thought or feeling but the awareness in which the whole stream is happening, the screen on which the film plays.",
 "The stream runs in time; the awareness is what it happens in","Part 1"),
mc("A","A meditator finds that a quiet gap between thoughts now shows up at red lights and between rooms, and that it feels full, not empty. Which view fits Conversation 5.7?",
 ["Her practice has produced a new state, which she must now work to keep going.","The gap is a sign that her mind is going blank, and she should bring it back to thought.","The quiet is only tiredness, and it will pass once she has rested and eaten.","She has lowered the noise until a presence that was always there could be felt."],3,
 "You do not produce the awareness; you stop drowning it out. When the practices quiet the stream, the noise floor drops and what was always there becomes noticeable, often as a gap that contains a quiet presence and not only an absence.",
 "The practices lower the noise; they do not make the awareness","Part 1"),
mc("A","A man says, “I have been told a hundred times that awareness is always here, but it never lands.” What does Conversation 5.7 suggest?",
 ["He needs to hear the words again, from a teacher who can say them better.","The stream is too loud: set it in order first, and the awareness becomes noticeable.","He lacks the faith that the teaching needs, and he should try to believe it first.","He should search harder, since a thing so near will only be found by effort."],1,
 "When the stream is loud and chaotic, the silent awareness is masked by noise, and no words can make it land, though it cannot be absent. The four practices do two jobs: they set the stream in order, and they quietly uncover the screen once the stream is calm enough.",
 "Words do not land in a loud stream; quiet it, and the awareness shows","Part 1"),
judge("“The old map of the inner ascent, with its channel and its stations, is best taken as literal anatomy that a surgeon could find.”",F,
 "Swamiji says plainly that the map is not literal anatomy: no surgeon finds these channels with a scalpel. It is an experiential map, a description of stages that practitioners report passing through in a strikingly consistent order. Hold it lightly, as a traveller holds an old chart, and check it against your own experience.",
 "The map is an experiential chart, not literal anatomy","Part 2"),
mc("A","A student asks the teacher to draw the source itself as one more box on the map. What does Conversation 5.7 say?",
 ["The old map is lost, and no later teacher has managed to rebuild it.","The source lies too far above the mind for any teacher to put into words.","Maps grasp objects, and the awareness doing the grasping cannot be one of them.","The source is kept secret, and is shared only with those who are prepared."],2,
 "Every map, equation and concept is a way the mind grasps things as objects. The awareness pointed to is the very thing doing the grasping, so the moment it becomes an object you hold only a thought about it. A map that claimed to contain the mapmaker would disprove itself.",
 "Maps grasp objects; the grasper cannot be one of them","Part 2"),
mc("A","A lecturer announces that she has grasped the ultimate completely and can explain it in full. How does Conversation 5.7 read that claim?",
 ["As the surest sign that her learning is deep, since only a master claims so much.","As a claim that deserves respect, because few people dare to make it plainly.","As a sign that she has made it an object: one more thing held in awareness.","As a sign that she has finished the staircase and has nothing left to climb."],2,
 "The Upaniṣadic paradox is that it is unknown to those who claim to know it and known to those who do not. A claim to have grasped the ultimate turns it into an object, one more thing appearing in awareness. The honest knower says: I cannot make it an object, I can only be it.",
 "Claiming to have grasped the ultimate makes it an object again","Part 2"),
judge("“A brain scan can show that meditation quiets the mind’s self-referential chatter, but no scan can detect the awareness in which the scanning appears.”",T,
 "Dr Watson reports real correlates: the default mode network quiets and brainwave synchronisation appears. But every instrument measures a signal that changes in time, within the stream, while awareness is that in which all measurement appears. It is like asking what is north of the North Pole, not a matter of a better device.",
 "Scans reach the mind in time, but not the awareness it appears in","Part 3"),
mc("A","A reader says that science can be ignored because it cannot reach awareness, and another says that only what a scan can show is real. How does Conversation 5.7 reply?",
 ["Both are half right, and each should choose the lens that suits their temperament.","The first is right, since awareness is the deeper matter and science only the surface.","Hold both: test what can be tested, and receive what can only be received.","The second is right, since anything beyond measurement cannot be spoken of at all."],2,
 "Insist only on the scan and you dismiss the deepest thing as unprovable and miss it; dismiss the science and you drift into ungrounded fantasy. Swamiji’s posture is: tested where it can be tested, received where it can only be received, and honest about which is which.",
 "Use both lenses, each in its proper place","Part 3"),
mc("U","What does Conversation 5.7 say about who does the climbing in the inner ascent?",
 ["The practitioner alone, whose effort builds the pathway one step at a time here.","A power outside the person, which comes down on the few who have earned it.","A teacher, who lifts the student step by step and keeps the way open for them.","Consciousness itself, returning to know its own nature and waking up as the person."],3,
 "In the view of the contemplative traditions the rising power is consciousness returning to recognise itself, using a pathway built into us for the purpose. So it is not an upgrade you achieve: you are not climbing toward it; it is waking up as you. That takes the strain out of the whole endeavour.",
 "Consciousness is waking up as you; you are not climbing toward it","Part 3"),
judge("“The course is a staircase that is good for climbing, so the staircase is the roof, and the course is the awakening itself.”",F,
 "The staircase is genuinely built for climbing, but it is not the roof: no map is the territory and no description is the thing described. To polish the steps forever and never climb would miss the point. Yet the stairs are made of the same material as the roof, so the climber has been within it all along.",
 "The staircase is for climbing; it is not the roof","Part 3")])
